1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,750
Automation scripts can expose credentials,

2
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:04,550
send messages without approval,

3
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:06,730
or fail because of incorrect expressions.

4
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:09,090
For developers building these workflows,

5
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:11,190
reviewing every change takes time.

6
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:13,850
CodeSentinel helps them find configured risks,

7
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:15,280
inspect a proposed repair,

8
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:17,490
and review the evidence before using the result.

9
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,830
Let’s start with this payment webhook.

10
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:22,530
This interface demonstration uses simulation,

11
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:24,150
clearly labelled on screen.

12
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:26,820
We’ll show a separate, real NVIDIA response

13
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:27,950
from Nebius afterwards.

14
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:30,580
Clicking Analyze code reveals three findings:

15
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:33,810
a hardcoded credential, a missing human approval gate,

16
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:35,310
and an incorrect expression.

17
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:38,450
The initial rule score is 45 out of 100.

18
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:40,180
Repair source proposes changes

19
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:41,810
and rechecks the configured rules.

20
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:44,670
In this simulated example, the score reaches 100.

21
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:47,110
The comparison shows exactly what changed:

22
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:49,530
the credential moves to an environment variable,

23
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:51,070
the expression is corrected,

24
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:54,050
and an approval check is added before sending the notification.

25
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:56,730
That score reflects the configured checks.

26
00:00:57,180 --> 00:00:58,640
It does not prove that the application

27
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:00,200
behaves correctly at runtime.

28
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:03,410
CodeSentinel does not execute the proposed code.

29
00:01:03,780 --> 00:01:06,190
Repair proposals also face structural safeguards.

30
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:08,480
These 14 targeted tests cover cases

31
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:11,270
including blank proposals, deleted exported functions,

32
00:01:11,440 --> 00:01:13,590
and changes to supported function signatures.

33
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:16,530
Incomplete repairs must remain marked as partial.

34
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:18,920
Now, here is our saved real response

35
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:21,750
from NVIDIA Nemotron Super on Nebius Token Factory.

36
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:24,560
The model replaced a fictitious demonstration credential

37
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:25,970
with an environment variable.

38
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:28,450
The returned repair passed static rules,

39
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:30,610
TypeScript checks, and controlled fixture checks.

40
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:33,380
The provider response identifier and token usage

41
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:35,130
are recorded alongside the result.

42
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:37,180
This is previously recorded evidence,

43
00:01:37,180 --> 00:01:39,670
separate from the simulated interface demonstration.

44
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:41,830
CodeSentinel brings repair proposals,

45
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:43,980
deterministic checks, and reviewable evidence

46
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:44,860
into one workspace,

47
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:46,710
while keeping the developer in control.
